vine 1.2.0
prefix: sen
title: Scout minimal surface and encapsulation
---
[sen] Scout's minimal public surface satisfies every known use, packaged and encapsulated (planning)
Scout today is a source-managed search engine with a legacy reader still
attached, a transition apparatus with nothing left to transition, a public
surface whose entries overlap in meaning, and a web-search tool bundled in for
no reason beyond one MCP server being convenient.
The destination is a Scout whose public surface is the smallest one that
satisfies every use we actually have, whose necessary features carry no
machinery for features we do not, and which is packaged well enough that
extraction later is mechanical. localmodal is the first case study consumer.
Complete when every known use in the inventory is exercised against the ruled
minimal surface, and no measurement finds a feature serving no use.
-> sen/verify/uses-satisfied
> Every implementation step is performed by a dispatched Claude Opus 5 subagent. The orchestrator maintains this graph, reviews returned work, runs integration checks, and owns every commit; subagents neither stage nor commit.
> A dispatch prompt is an address, not a briefing: it names this file and one node id. Every fact the agent needs lives in that node and in this root's attachments, because a thin prompt over a complete node beats the reverse.
> Nodes own disjoint file sets. Two may be dispatched concurrently only when their owned files and their acceptance commands are both disjoint.
> localmodal is the only workspace Scout serves in this horizon. One-workspace assumptions are permitted, and each is stated at the point it is made so a later multi-workspace need finds them.
> Web search separates completely: nothing in the source engine imports it, and nothing in it imports the source engine.
> Necessity precedes layout, carried by dependency edges rather than by advice.
> Every instrument this graph produces carries a known-answer fixture and reports nothing when that fixture fails.
> The graph ends at ready-to-extract: installable and encapsulated, still living in this repository.
> The public surface is the MCP tools, the CLI subcommands, and the two process protocols the workers speak. Anything else is internal. This definition is stated here so no node has to reconstruct it from an attachment.
> Subagents do not modify this file. Status, artifacts, and structure are recorded by the orchestrator after review, so a returned result and the graph's account of it never disagree.
@guidance text/markdown ../planning.md
@guidance text/markdown scout-organization.md
@guidance text/markdown scout-vocabulary.md
---
[sen/measure/use-inventory] Inventory every known use of Scout (reviewing)
"Satisfied for all known uses" is only checkable against a written list, and no
such list exists.
Owns: proposals/scout-uses.md (new).
Do: enumerate every caller of Scout in this repository and its practice. Cover
the MCP tools as invoked from VS Code, every CLI subcommand, the smoke suite,
and the research workflows in localmodal.vine that cite Scout for provenance.
For each use record: who invokes it, through which entry point, what it needs
back, and what it would cost if that use disappeared.
Report data, not judgment: no use is ranked or retired here.
Accept: every public entry point in the tree appears against at least one named
use, or is listed explicitly as having none.
@artifact text/markdown scout-uses.md
---
[sen/measure/surface-overlap] Map overlapping semantics in the public surface (reviewing)
The surface has entries that mean the same thing in different clothes. Known
starting points, to be confirmed rather than assumed: with activation dead,
bootstrap is propose against an empty store; refresh-stale is propose with a
selection predicate instead of caller-supplied rows; the CLI and MCP surfaces
are two spellings of one dispatch; source_read resolves what scout_search
returns.
Owns: proposals/scout-surface-overlap.md (new).
Do: for every public entry point, state its operation in terms of the source
model, then group entries whose operations are the same modulo an argument or a
default. For each group give the collapsed form and what would be lost.
Accept: the overlap map covers the whole surface, and each proposed collapse
names what a caller could no longer express.
@artifact text/markdown scout-surface-overlap.md
---
[sen/design/minimal-surface] Design with user: rule the minimal surface (planning)
Given the use inventory and the overlap map, rule the surface: which entry
points exist, which collapse into which, and which argument or default carries
the difference that used to be a separate name.
Accept: the owner rules one surface; every entry traces to a use in the
inventory, and every collapsed entry names the use that still reaches it.
-> sen/measure/surface-overlap
-> sen/measure/use-inventory
---
[sen/design/web-boundary] Design with user: where web search separates to (planning)
Direction is ruled: web search separates completely. The shape is open. It
measures as 661 lines across four modules touching the source engine at zero
points, so the cost of separating is already near zero; what remains is where it
lands and how it is served.
Candidate shapes, sized rather than recommended: its own package with its own
entry point inside this repository; its own MCP server process configured
separately in .vscode/mcp.json; or its own distribution entirely.
Accept: the owner rules one shape, and the ruling states whether the two are
ever served from one process again.
-> sen/measure/use-inventory
---
[sen/design/root-provenance] Design with user: where a root comes from (planning)
29 of 53 modules derive their own location from __file__. Scout also has more
than one root: the repository root that repo-file origins and VINE citations
resolve against, the state root holding ledger, publications and artifacts, and
the location of the checked-in configuration. Serving one workspace lets these
coincide, which is permitted here, but the count is what needs ruling before a
mechanism is chosen.
Accept: the owner states how many roots there are and how each reaches the code
that needs it, such that a module's physical position stops being load-bearing.
---
[sen/remove/legacy-readers] Retire the pre-activation reader path (notstarted)
Owns: resources/search.py, resources/freshness.py, resources/sources.json, and
the legacy alias routing plus the unconditional startup spawn in
scout/server.py.
Do: delete the pre-activation reader entirely. search.py carries a second
CURRENT pointer the source design ruled must never be read as truth, and
server.py spawns its worker at startup on every run, paying an index and model
load for a route that resolves elsewhere.
Accept: no alias resolves through a pre-activation index, no second index
pointer exists in the tree, and starting the server loads no legacy worker.
-> sen/design/minimal-surface
---
[sen/remove/vendor-fetchers] Retire the per-vendor fetch scripts (notstarted)
Owns: resources/fetch_modal_docs.py, resources/fetch_papers.py,
resources/fetch_vscode_docs.py.
Do: delete all three. Their onboarding role was replaced by the public
propose and refresh paths; each is a bespoke script standing in for one
declaration.
Accept: no onboarding path requires a bespoke vendor script.
-> sen/design/minimal-surface
---
[sen/remove/legacy-migration] Retire the adopt-in-place import path (notstarted)
Owns: resources/source_migration.py, resources/initial-sources.json, the
migrate and write-migration-manifest CLI subcommands, and the
SourceControl.bootstrap_import path with the imports map it consumes.
Do: delete the module and its entry points. Its whole reason to exist is the
imports map, which binds each row to an already-fetched local file so bytes can
be adopted instead of re-fetched — an optimisation for material that is 285
re-fetchable public URLs. Preserve the manifest's rows array first, which
sen/implement/corpus-rows owns.
Accept: no adopt-in-place materialization path remains, and no surviving command
reads the legacy manifest.
-> sen/implement/corpus-rows
---
[sen/remove/activation] Remove the activation concept (notstarted)
Owns: resources/activation.py and every reference to transition_lock,
is_source_control_active, and the ACTIVATED marker across the tree, plus the
BOOTSTRAP_AFTER_ACTIVATION and LEGACY_MIGRATION_REQUIRED members of the closed
diagnostic union.
Do: delete the cutover concept. Measured 2026-07-24 it is 69 reference sites and
the only module reachable from all six public capabilities, and its sole job is
sequencing a transition whose other side no longer exists. An absent CURRENT
becomes an empty store rather than a pre-cutover state.
Accept: the diagnostic union is closed over surviving codes, no surviving module
imports a cutover concept, and an empty store reports as empty.
-> sen/remove/legacy-migration
-> sen/remove/legacy-readers
-> sen/remove/vendor-fetchers
---
[sen/remove/cached-corpus] Retire the checked-in corpus cache (notstarted)
Owns: resources/modal-docs/, resources/vscode-docs/, resources/pdf/.
Do: delete all three. The first two are a checked-in cache of material that a
declared source materializes, so holding them is a redundant description.
resources/pdf/ is different and must be reported rather than quietly dropped: no
declaration covers it, because v1 sources admit only text/plain and
text/markdown, so removing the legacy reader ends PDF search outright. Papers
survive as resources/papers.md, which is a declared repo-file row.
Accept: no checked-in directory duplicates material a declared source
materializes, and the ended coverage is stated in the returned report.
-> sen/implement/corpus-rows
---
[sen/remove/transition-tests] Retire transition-only coverage (notstarted)
Owns: scout/tests/test_corpus.py, test_freshness.py,
test_legacy_activation_guard.py, test_activation_marker.py, and the legacy leg
of test_machine_output.py.
Do: delete coverage whose subject no longer exists. Before deleting each file,
check whether any assertion in it covers surviving behaviour; if so, report it
rather than moving it, so its new home is a decision and not a side effect.
Accept: the suite covers only surviving behaviour, and the report names every
assertion that was carrying surviving coverage.
-> sen/remove/activation
-> sen/remove/cached-corpus
---
[sen/implement/corpus-rows] Publish the corpus from ordinary declarations (notstarted)
Owns: resources/scout-sources.json (new, the checked-in row array).
Do: lift the rows array out of the migration manifest — 292 rows, 285 https and
7 repo-file — as ordinary add declarations, and build the store from them
through the ordinary path. Re-fetching is not a fallback here but the better
result: current content, real digests, real TTL semantics, rather than adopted
stale bytes.
Accept: the corpus is queryable from a publication built by ordinary
materialization, with no import-adoption path involved, and the row array is
checked in as ordinary declaration input.
-> sen/design/minimal-surface
---
[sen/implement/surface-collapse] Apply the ruled surface (notstarted)
Owns: resources/source_cli.py, resources/source_worker.py, and the tool
registrations in scout/server.py.
Do: implement the ruled minimal surface, collapsing entries that the overlap map
showed to be one operation in two spellings, and describing the operation-to-
method mapping once rather than once per surface. Today source_cli.main and
source_worker._reply are two dispatch tables over the same operations with
separately written validation and error rendering.
Accept: changing an operation is a single-site edit, both surfaces return the
shared control-plane result shape, and every surviving entry traces to a use in
the inventory.
-> sen/design/minimal-surface
-> sen/remove/transition-tests
---
[sen/implement/web-separation] Separate web search to its ruled home (notstarted)
Owns: scout/sanitize.py, scout/creds.py, and the web tooling in
scout/server.py.
Do: move web search to the shape the owner ruled, so that the source engine
carries no dependency on Gemini, credential storage, or the sanitizer, and web
search carries no dependency on the publication store. Its sanitization
guarantees are load-bearing and travel with it unchanged: unicode hygiene,
structure stripping, budget, untrusted labelling, and citations taken from
grounding metadata rather than model text.
Accept: neither side imports the other, the sanitizer's behaviour is unchanged
under its existing tests, and each is startable without the other's
configuration.
-> sen/design/web-boundary
-> sen/remove/transition-tests
---
[sen/implement/one-name-per-thing] Give each shared name one owner (notstarted)
Owns: resources/source_index.py, resources/vine.py, and the diagnostic repair
strings in resources/diagnostics.py.
Do: three facts are currently described more than once. The embedding model is
declared independently in source_index.py and vine.py, and the VINE windower
derives its token budget from the identity it declares for itself, so a silent
divergence produces segments exceeding the encoder limit the index actually
uses. Diagnostic repair strings name invocation paths, so the closed error union
carries knowledge of the file layout. Give each fact one owner and let the other
sites read it.
Accept: one declaration of the embedding identity with the windower's budget
derived from it, and a repair instruction that survives a layout change without
edits.
-> sen/remove/transition-tests
---
[sen/implement/root-provenance] Remove derived location from non-entrypoints (notstarted)
Owns: resources/config.py, scout/tests/harness.py, and the __file__ rebinds
across scout/tests/*.py.
Do: apply the ruled provenance. Eighteen test modules currently rebind __file__
to scout/smoke.py so that parents[1] arithmetic still reaches the repository
root, and test_ledger.py derives a subprocess PYTHONPATH from that rebound
value; config.py addresses the checked-in configuration by its own code
position.
Accept: no module outside the ruled deriving set references __file__ for
location, and the suite passes with no module asserting a false position.
-> sen/design/root-provenance
---
[sen/implement/public-surface-tests] Rebind tests to the public surface (notstarted)
Owns: resources/source_control/__init__.py,
resources/source_control/plane.py, resources/source_control/execution.py, and
scout/tests/test_source_control.py.
Do: the owner ruled that tests address the public surface only. Today the
package re-exports its surface and owns runtime seams that the suite patches on
the module object, and plane.py and execution.py resolve the package through
sys.modules[__package__] at call time to keep those patches effective —
reproducing a pre-split single-file namespace. Remove the self-reference and
rebind the failure-injection fixtures to reach through the public surface.
Accept: no test addresses a module object, no module resolves its own package at
call time, and every failure previously injected by patching is still exercised.
-> sen/design/test-coupling-applies
-> sen/implement/root-provenance
---
[sen/design/test-coupling-applies] Record the ruled test-addressable surface (complete)
The owner ruled 2026-07-24 that tests address the public surface only: no
module-object patching and no internals. This node carries the ruling for the
nodes that apply it.
> Tests address the public surface only; no module-object patching and no internals.
> The sys.modules[__package__] self-reference and the package's seam ownership have no surviving justification.
> Failure injection that relied on patching a module object becomes reachable through the public surface or through a fixture.
---
[sen/implement/instrument-fixtures] Give the measurement scripts known-answer fixtures (reviewing)
Owns: tools/ (new).
Do: the import-graph and location-census scripts used to produce
proposals/scout-organization.md live in a temp directory and twice reported
confident structure from silently dropped edges. Give them a home and a fixture
whose answer is known by hand — a small tree containing one deliberate cycle,
one package __init__ with relative imports, and one module deriving its own
location — and have each script report nothing when its fixture fails.
Accept: each instrument passes its fixture before printing, and reintroducing
either historical bug makes the fixture fail.
@artifact text/x-python ../tools/import_graph.py
@artifact text/x-python ../tools/location_census.py
@artifact text/x-python ../tools/fixture.py
---
[sen/implement/location-check] Make location-dependence a standing check (notstarted)
Owns: the location census entry point under tools/.
Do: give the census a threshold and a home in the suite, so a module deriving
its own location outside the ruled set fails rather than waiting to be found by
the next refactor.
Accept: introducing a derived location outside the ruled set fails the check.
-> sen/implement/instrument-fixtures
-> sen/implement/root-provenance
---
[sen/design/package-layout] Design with user: the package that remains (planning)
With the surface ruled, the removals landed, web search separated, and location
no longer load-bearing, choose the layout: whether resources/ and scout/ become
one package, where the checked-in configuration lives once Scout is installable,
what the distribution's entry points are named, and whether the source worker is
a peer process or an internal detail.
Measured inputs: the import graph is 8 strata with zero static cycles, so the
layering is real but invisible in the tree; four disciplines currently reach the
same code — bare-name flat namespace, package-relative imports, importlib by
file path, and subprocess spawn by absolute path.
Accept: the owner selects one layout, and every surviving module has exactly one
home under it.
-> sen/implement/one-name-per-thing
-> sen/implement/public-surface-tests
-> sen/implement/surface-collapse
-> sen/implement/web-separation
---
[sen/implement/package-layout] Move to the ruled layout under one import discipline (notstarted)
Owns: the tree move itself, and pyproject.toml (new).
Do: apply the selected layout as one change, collapsing the four ways a caller
reaches engine code into one, and make the result installable with declared
entry points so extraction later is a repository move rather than a redesign.
Accept: one import discipline holds across the tree, the suite is green before
and after, no spawn target is addressed by a path computed from a module's own
position, and a clean install exposes the ruled entry points.
-> sen/design/package-layout
-> sen/implement/location-check
-> sen/remove/cached-corpus
---
[sen/verify/uses-satisfied] Verify every known use against the minimal surface (notstarted)
Owns: the verification report and the suite's integration leg.
Do: exercise every use in the inventory end to end against the packaged Scout,
through the surfaces the owner ruled. Re-run the capability, strata, location,
and name-ownership measurements and record the deltas beside the 2026-07-24
baseline in proposals/scout-organization.md.
Report data: per-use pass or fail with the command that produced it, measured
module and line counts per capability, and the surviving diagnostic union.
Accept: every inventoried use is satisfied, no measurement finds a feature
serving no use, and no measured claim rests on an instrument without a passing
fixture.
-> sen/implement/package-layout
